\section{Discussion}
\label{sec:discussion}

\paragraph{Why the router fails: task-type-level signal.}
The router diagnostic (\S\ref{sec:router-diagnostic}) reveals a structural mismatch: death avoidance is a task-type-level binary property (10/11 types carry death risk), but step-level features correlate weakly because death risk depends on multi-step trajectories, not individual action semantics. The 4B router improves at episode level ($59.0\%$) without step-level gains ($56$--$59\%$), confirming task-type classification rather than generalizable features; cross-task-type transfer yields chance accuracy (49.2\%; Appendix~\ref{app:ablations}).

\paragraph{Non-triviality of death dominance.}
While death dominance follows from the penalty structure (\S\ref{sec:daf}), three properties were not predictable a priori: (i)~\emph{routing prior}---the oracle matches always-deterministic on 96.3\% of decisions (2.89-point improvement), and threshold routing with perfect death risk knowledge still cannot outperform always-deterministic (Appendix~\ref{sec:threshold-routing}), so the rational prior is ``do not route''; (ii)~\emph{intervention resistance}---retry ($43.5\%$), Reflexion ($34.0\%$), $10{\times}$ scaling ($30.6\%$), and action-space filtering ($29.0\%$) all leave ${\geq}$12~pp residual gaps to deterministic safety ($16.3\%$), with Reflexion and scaling converging to overlapping Wilson CIs suggesting a shared bound from lacking ground-truth state access; (iii)~\emph{cross-model stability}---Qwen-7B and Llama-8B show nearly identical internal vulnerability (${\sim}$42\%), and 72B scaling leaves death dominance intact (DAF $= 92.4\%$). More aggressive interventions (Tree-of-Thought~\citep{tot-2023}, process reward models) remain untested; we characterize a ceiling for the tested classes, not a universal bound.

\paragraph{Anatomy of catastrophic failure.}
All 105 internal-strategy deaths trace to misuse of \texttt{focus on [object]}, the answer-submission action, as a general interaction command (Appendix~\ref{app:failure-modes}). The root cause is grounding failure: agents parse task descriptions correctly but cannot map understanding to valid primitives; the majority of death episodes show effective reasoning in non-fatal steps before a single grounding error triggers irreversible failure. The internal strategy's higher death count (105 vs.\ 40) reflects more frequent entry into catastrophic states, not different within-episode behavior (${\sim}5\%$ \texttt{focus on} rate in both). \texttt{find-non-living-thing} (zero deaths) uses \texttt{focus on} more frequently than death-prone tasks---non-living objects are the majority class, so indiscriminate submissions rarely trigger death; lethality is a joint function of action misuse and answer distribution. Direct grounding interventions reduce but do not eliminate vulnerability ($48.0\% \to 29.0\%$ via action-space filter; Appendix~\ref{app:grounding-ablation}).

\paragraph{Practitioner implications.}
A three-stage diagnostic (${\sim}$50-episode pilot $\to$ death-rate differential $\to$ death-label fraction; Appendix~\ref{app:decision-framework}) correctly predicts outcomes for all four conditions (Qwen-7B, Llama-8B, Qwen-72B, MATH). Our router training consumed ${\sim}$200 GPU-hours without exceeding always-deterministic; this diagnostic could have predicted that from a 50-episode pilot. Counterfactual labels also derive from pure-strategy episodes; the 4B router's end-to-end score ($-12.03$, below always-deterministic $-9.47$) may partly reflect distribution shift at deployment~\citep{dagger-2011}.

\paragraph{Framework applicability.}
The penalty-independent label fraction (78.9\%) is the primary diagnostic; DAF increases monotonically with $P$ (Appendix~\ref{app:penalty}). Preliminary pilots on MiniHack~\citep{minihack-2021} (0\% death, no monster spawning) and Crafter~\citep{crafter-2022} (100\% death, both strategies) produced no death asymmetry, illustrating two boundary conditions: environments lacking a death mechanism yield no routing signal (consistent with MATH), while environments where no strategy avoids death lack exploitable asymmetry. The framework requires an intermediate regime where strategy choice affects survival; NetHack~\citep{nethack-2020} combat tasks remain the most direct replication target \citep{balrog-2025,netplay-2024}.

\section{Experiments}

We analyze 11 of ScienceWorld's 30 task types (245 episodes); the 19 excluded types (63\%) lack actionable routing signal due to development contamination (5), taxonomic redundancy (7), or agent-floor performance (7). This scope limits generalizability: conclusions apply to the included subset, not to all ScienceWorld tasks. An anti-cherry-picking analysis confirms that death dominance holds in 18 of these 19 types, with a mean $\Delta\text{DR} = +38.2$\,pp exceeding the included tasks' $+28.7$\,pp (Appendix~\ref{app:per-task}).

\subsection{Death Avoidance Decomposition}
\label{sec:death-decomp}

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.85\columnwidth]{figures/fig1_daf_scatter.pdf}
\caption{Per-task scatter: death rate differential (internal $-$ deterministic) vs.\ performance gap ($\Delta_{\text{full}}$). $r = 0.97$, $p < 10^{-6}$, $n = 11$ task types. Since $\Delta_{\text{full}} \approx \delta P + \Delta_{\text{nd}}$ with $P$ constant, $r \to 1$ as DAF $\to 1$; the near-perfect correlation is a structural consequence of death dominance, not an independent finding (\S\ref{sec:death-decomp}).}
\label{fig:decomposition}
\end{figure}

Of 95 non-tie routing labels across 245 episodes, 75 (78.9\%) arise from death asymmetry: one strategy dies while the other survives. This label fraction is penalty-independent---it depends only on which episodes produce asymmetric deaths, not on how death is penalized. Of these 75, 54 are sole-signal labels (episodes where only one strategy produced a non-zero score): both strategies score zero without death, so death avoidance was the \emph{sole} discriminating signal. The remaining 21 death-asymmetry labels involve episodes where the surviving strategy also earned positive score. At $P{=}0$, the 54 sole-signal labels reduce to ties, leaving 41 non-tie labels of which 21 (51.2\%) still involve death asymmetry; the labeling function itself is penalty-independent (Algorithm~\ref{alg:labeling}), but the practical routing value of death avoidance requires $P > 0$.

Under ScienceWorld's penalty structure ($P{=}100$), the full score gap is $\Delta_{\text{full}} = +29.60$; excluding death episodes, $\Delta_{\text{nd}} = +2.84$, yielding DAF $= 90.4\%$ (95\% CI: $[81.6\%, 98.8\%]$, episode-level bootstrap, $B{=}10{,}000$). DAF varies with penalty magnitude ($50.4\%$ at $P{=}10$; $70.8\%$ at $P{=}25$; $82.6\%$ at $P{=}50$; $90.4\%$ at $P{=}100$; Appendix~\ref{app:penalty}), confirming that the 78.9\% label fraction provides the more robust, penalty-independent characterization. Given $\delta = 0.265$ and $P = 100$, the predicted DAF $= \delta P / (\delta P + \Delta_{\text{nd}}) = 26.5 / 29.37 \approx 90.3\%$, closely matching the observed value; DAF is thus a derived quantity once $\delta$, $P$, and $\Delta_{\text{nd}}$ are known.

\paragraph{Per-task correlation structure.}
The internal strategy dies in 42.9\% of episodes versus 16.3\% for deterministic (2.6$\times$), consistent with the internal strategy's reliance on self-generated observations that compound prediction errors across steps. Per-task, death rate differential correlates with $\Delta_{\text{full}}$ ($r = 0.97$, $p < 10^{-6}$; Figure~\ref{fig:decomposition}). Since $P$ is constant across all task types, $\Delta_{\text{full}} = P \cdot \delta + \Delta_{\text{nd}}$ makes $\Delta_{\text{full}}$ a linear function of $\delta$ with residual $\Delta_{\text{nd}}$; when DAF $\rightarrow 1$, this residual vanishes and $r \rightarrow 1$ by construction. The near-perfect correlation is therefore a mathematical consequence of death dominance, not an independent empirical finding. We retain it as an internal consistency check. The correlation drops below significance at $P \leq 25$ (Appendix~\ref{app:penalty}). Of 245 episodes, 86 are \texttt{det\_preferred}, 9 \texttt{internal\_preferred}, and 150 \texttt{no\_preference}.

\paragraph{Signal concentration and binary structure.}
The Signal Concentration Index (SCI $= 0.2147$; Appendix~\ref{app:sci}) confirms low concentration: the top-3 task types account for only $39.8\%$ of total death-rate differential. The aggregate 78.9\% reflects a per-task binary structure: all 10 death-prone task types produce 100\% death-asymmetry labels, while \texttt{find-non-living-thing} (zero deaths, $\Delta = +9.58$ from non-death scores) produces 0\%. The framework thus diagnoses a binary property (``is this environment death-dominated?'') rather than a continuous routing signal. Removing \texttt{find-non-living-thing} raises the label fraction to 100\% and DAF to 96.8\% (Appendix~\ref{app:loto-daf}).

\paragraph{Sensitivity and seed robustness.}
Episode-level bootstrap ($B = 10{,}000$) yields 95\% CI $[81.6\%, 98.8\%]$ for DAF with Cohen's $d = 3.02$, indicating a large effect size; leave-one-task-out analysis confirms stability (DAF range: 75.9\%--78.9\%; Appendix~\ref{app:loto-daf}). An independent seed replication ($N{=}220$) yields DAF $= 89.7\%$, within 0.7~pp of the primary analysis (Appendix~\ref{app:seed-robustness}). As a conservative sensitivity analysis, cluster-robust inference treating the $K{=}11$ task types as clusters ($p < 0.001$) confirms significance even under within-cluster correlation; 28-task directional consistency (100\%) provides further support (Appendices~\ref{app:wild-cluster}--\ref{app:per-task-ci}).

\subsection{Multi-Model Validation}
\label{sec:cross-model}

A Llama-3.1-8B-Instruct~\citep{llama3-2024} cross-model replication across all 11 task types ($N{=}220$) confirms that death dominance replicates across model families: internal death rate $41.4\%$ vs.\ deterministic $11.4\%$ (death-rate differential $+30.0$~pp), with direction consistency 10/10 on death-prone tasks, 83 deterministic-preferred, 3 internal-preferred (1.4\%, all death-asymmetry reversals), and 134 no-preference labels.
Both models exhibit nearly identical internal vulnerability (${\sim}$42\%: Qwen $42.9\%$, Llama $41.4\%$), confirming that death-prone failure is structurally determined; the differential magnitude varies ($+26.5$~pp Qwen, $+30.0$~pp Llama) because deterministic strategy safety is model-dependent ($16.3\%$ vs.\ $11.4\%$).
\texttt{identify-life-stages-1} shows cross-model precision matching ($55\%$/$10\%$/$+45$~pp in both models).
\texttt{find-non-living-thing} shows zero deaths in both models, consistent with its majority-class answer distribution (\S\ref{sec:discussion}). Both models are in the 7--8B range; $10{\times}$ scaling (\S\ref{sec:agent-improvement}) confirms that death dominance persists across capability levels (Appendix~\ref{app:cross-model}).

\subsection{Agent Improvement Baselines}
\label{sec:agent-improvement}

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\begin{tabular}{@{}lrr@{}}
\toprule
Condition & Death\% & $N$ \\
\midrule
Single-trial baseline & 42.9 & 245 \\
Reflexion ($3{\times}$) & 31.4 & 220 \\
Qwen2.5-72B ($10{\times}$) & 30.6 & 255 \\
Deterministic baseline & 16.3 & 245 \\
\midrule
\multicolumn{3}{@{}l}{\textit{10 death-prone tasks only:}} \\
Death-prone baseline & 48.0 & 200 \\
Retry ($5{\times}$) & 43.5 & 200 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\caption{Internal-strategy death rates under agent improvement methods. Top: all 11 tasks (72B: independently collected at both internal and deterministic). Bottom: 10 death-prone tasks (\texttt{find-non-living-thing} excluded, 0\% death at all scales). All interventions reduce death rate but none approaches deterministic-level safety.}
\label{tab:agent-improvement}
\end{table}

We test whether agent-level improvement reduces death dominance (Table~\ref{tab:agent-improvement}). Retry ($N{=}5$ attempts without self-correction) yields marginal reduction on death-prone tasks ($43.5\%$ vs.\ $48.0\%$ baseline, $\Delta = -4.5$~pp). Reflexion~\citep{reflexion-2023} (verbal self-reflection between trials) reduces death to 31.4\% across all 11 tasks by rescuing 29.3\% of first-trial deaths, but the gap to deterministic remains 15.1~pp; on the 10 death-prone tasks, Reflexion achieves ${\sim}$35\%. Under Reflexion ($N{=}3$, matched-episode analysis), $\Delta_{\text{nd}} = -0.47$, yielding DAF $> 100\%$: the deterministic advantage is entirely attributable to death avoidance, and in non-death episodes the self-correcting agent slightly outperforms the deterministic baseline. Scaling to Qwen2.5-72B ($10{\times}$ parameters; both internal and deterministic strategies independently collected at 72B, 255 episodes across all 11 task types) reduces death rate to $30.6\%$. The 72B deterministic strategy achieves $2.75\%$ death rate ($\Delta\text{DR} = +27.8$~pp), and $\Delta_{\text{nd}} = +2.28$, yielding DAF $= 92.4\%$ (7B: $90.4\%$); scaling lowers death rate but leaves death dominance intact.

\paragraph{Dose-response analysis.}
We extend both interventions to $N{=}5$ trials on death-prone tasks ($n{=}200$; Figure~\ref{fig:dose-response}). Retry is nearly flat ($48.0\% \to 43.5\%$, $\Delta = -4.5$~pp over five trials). Reflexion drops steeply to $34.0\%$ at $N{=}3$ ($\Delta = -13.5$~pp, $z = 2.75$, $p = 0.003$) then plateaus ($34$--$36.5\%$ for $N \geq 3$, pairwise $p > 0.5$); the minimum detectable effect at $n{=}200$ is ${\sim}14$~pp ($\alpha = 0.05$, 80\% power), indicating adequate sensitivity for the observed $N{=}1 \to 3$ reduction but not for smaller plateau-region changes. Retry's per-trial conditional death rate stays constant at ${\sim}48\%$; Reflexion's declines to ${\sim}31\%$ by Trial~3 (per-task breakdown in Appendix~\ref{app:dose-response}).

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{figures/dose_response.pdf}
\caption{Death rate as a function of trial count ($N$) for Retry and Reflexion on 10 death-prone tasks ($n{=}200$). Retry (blue) is nearly flat; Reflexion (red) drops steeply through $N{=}3$ then plateaus. Horizontal lines: 72B scaling baseline ($30.6\%$, all 11 tasks) and deterministic baseline ($16.3\%$). Error bands: Wilson 95\% CIs.}
\label{fig:dose-response}
\end{figure}

\subsection{Cross-Environment Boundary Probes}
\label{sec:math-contrast}
\label{sec:cross-env}

We evaluate two additional environments as boundary probes (Appendix~\ref{app:cross-env-details}). ``Deterministic'' denotes qualitatively different computation modes per environment: ScienceWorld uses an oracle simulator with ground-truth state, MATH uses a Python code interpreter, and ALFWorld uses admissible-action enumeration. These are not equivalent; results probe framework applicability rather than replicate findings.

\paragraph{MATH.} On MATH Level~4--5~\citep{math-2021} ($N{=}795$, code interpreter as deterministic computation mode), internal reasoning and code interpretation achieve near-identical accuracy ($73.5\%$ vs.\ $73.0\%$, McNemar $p = 0.81$, 95\% CI $[-2.6, +3.6]$~pp), with balanced labels (107 vs.\ 95, 74.6\% no-preference). Without catastrophic failure, no routing signal emerges.

\paragraph{ALFWorld.} ALFWorld~\citep{alfworld-2021} (6 task types, 135 games) provides the deterministic strategy with admissible actions, an information asymmetry rather than a computation mode switch. The success rate gap is $27.4\%$ vs.\ $3.0\%$ (35 det-preferred, 2 int-preferred, 98 no-preference). Of 37 non-tie labels, 35 (94.6\%) favor the information-advantaged strategy, a one-directional dominance analogous to ScienceWorld's death dominance but driven by information asymmetry rather than catastrophic failure. The framework diagnoses this qualitatively different mechanism without modification.

\subsection{Baselines}
\label{sec:baselines}

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\resizebox{\columnwidth}{!}{%
\begin{tabular}{@{}lccc@{}}
\toprule
Strategy & Score & 95\% CI & Death \% \\
\midrule
Always-Internal & $-39.07$ & [$-46.40$, $-32.27$] & 42.9 \\
Always-Deterministic & $-9.47$ & [$-14.71$, $-4.29$] & 16.3 \\
Random & $-22.38$ & [$-28.67$, $-15.75$] & 27.8 \\
Oracle & $-6.58$ & [$-11.54$, $-1.70$] & 14.3 \\
\midrule
Trained Router$^\dagger$ & $-10.70$ & [$-16.33$, $-5.27$] & 17.6 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}}
\caption{Episode-weighted mean scores on ScienceWorld (11 tasks, 245 episodes). $\dagger$\,Single random seed (42) per episode pair; see Appendix~\ref{app:seed-robustness} for 8-seed robustness analysis.}
\label{tab:baselines}
\end{table}

Always-deterministic ($-9.47$) outperforms always-internal ($-39.07$) by 29.60 points. The oracle improves only to $-6.58$, with 96.3\% of decisions matching always-deterministic---an \emph{oracle degeneracy} where an always-deterministic lookup achieves 90.5\% of non-tie oracle decisions (balanced accuracy 50.0\%: perfect deterministic recall, zero internal recall).

\subsection{Router Training as Diagnostic Probe}
\label{sec:router-diagnostic}

We probe whether counterfactual labels can train a step-level router as a diagnostic of label informativeness. The oracle margin (2.89 points) caps routing upside; at step level, 97.4\% of comparisons are ties and only 0.3\% of training steps conflict directionally with episode labels, leaving minimal step-level signal for the router to exploit. An implicit router (Qwen3, LoRA; Appendix~\ref{app:router-details}) trained at three scales (0.6B, 1.7B, 4B) with 4-seed configurations shows flat step-level balanced accuracy ($56$--$59\%$, within seed variance). At episode level, only 4B exceeds always-deterministic ($59.0 \pm 1.9\%$, $p < 0.005$; Appendix~\ref{app:ablations}); 0.6B and 1.7B remain at or below chance. The multi-step nature of routing (10--50 actions with compounding state uncertainty) creates inherent label noise between step-level decisions and episode-level outcomes. Training and validation use episode-level splits (Appendix~\ref{app:router-details}), ensuring no within-episode information leakage.

\paragraph{End-to-end deployment.} The 4B router deployed end-to-end ($N{=}245$) scores $-12.03$ (95\% CI: $[-17.82, -6.45]$), not exceeding always-deterministic ($-9.47$). Paraphrase-based augmentation ($25\%$--$800\%$) provides no improvement; the $3.7\%$ internal-preferred base rate makes real-data collection expensive (Appendices~\ref{app:seed-robustness}--\ref{app:ablations}).

\section{Method}
\label{sec:method}

Our diagnostic framework quantifies what drives routing signal in interactive environments through three components: (i)~episode-level counterfactual labeling that generates routing preferences from paired pure-strategy outcomes (\S\ref{sec:collection}--\ref{sec:labeling}); (ii)~the Death Avoidance Factor (DAF), which decomposes the routing score gap into death-avoidance and non-death components (\S\ref{sec:daf}); and (iii)~the Signal Concentration Index (SCI), which measures whether routing signal is concentrated in a few task types or distributed systematically (Appendix~\ref{app:sci}). Episode-level bootstrap resampling provides 95\% confidence intervals for all metrics.

\subsection{Problem Setup}
\label{sec:problem-setup}

Consider an interactive environment with observation space $\mathcal{O}$ and action space $\mathcal{A}$. An agent policy $\pi: \mathcal{O}^* \rightarrow \mathcal{A}$ maps observation histories to actions. We define two computation modes $\mathcal{M} = \{{\tt int}, {\tt det}\}$: \textbf{internal} (${\tt int}$), where the agent predicts intermediate observations via chain-of-thought reasoning (ReAct~\cite{react-2023}), with prediction errors compounding across steps; and \textbf{deterministic} (${\tt det}$), where the agent receives ground-truth observations from the environment simulator at each step. A \emph{routing policy} $\rho: \mathcal{O}^* \rightarrow \mathcal{M}$ selects which computation mode to use at each step; our analysis investigates what signal is available to learn $\rho$. A reasoning-depth ablation ($n{=}120$) found no significant difference ($p > 0.26$; Appendix~\ref{app:ablations}).

\subsection{Counterfactual Episode Collection}
\label{sec:collection}

Per-step oracle labeling assigns routing labels by comparing single-step outcomes under each computation modality. Under sparse terminal rewards, this approach degenerates: on ScienceWorld \cite{scienceworld-2022}, 97.4\% of steps produce identical intermediate scores regardless of modality (5{,}726 of 5{,}878 steps across 11 prescreened task types), yielding no per-step routing signal. The 97.4\% tie rate arises because ScienceWorld assigns rewards only at episode termination; intermediate steps update environment state but do not change the score, so a per-step oracle can only distinguish strategies through downstream consequences visible at episode end. Episode-level comparison is therefore the principled granularity for sparse-reward environments, not a fallback from per-step analysis: it directly measures whether one strategy produces better terminal outcomes than the other, bypassing the uninformative intermediate steps entirely.

\paragraph{Pure-strategy trajectories.}
For a task instance $t$ and modality $m \in \mathcal{M}$, a \emph{pure-strategy trajectory} is the complete episode obtained by applying modality $m$ at every step:
\begin{equation}
    \tau_m^{(t)} = (o_1, a_1, o_2, a_2, \ldots, o_T, a_T)
\end{equation}
where $T \leq T_{\max}$ is the episode length (capped at $T_{\max} = 30$ steps). Both trajectories $\tau_{\tt int}^{(t)}$ and $\tau_{\tt det}^{(t)}$ are executed under identical initial conditions (same random seed, same task variation), so performance differences arise solely from the computation mode choice.

Each trajectory $\tau$ yields a terminal score $s(\tau) \in \mathbb{Z}$ and a death indicator $d(\tau) \in \{0, 1\}$. When the agent dies ($d = 1$), the environment imposes a penalty: $s(\tau) = -P$ where $P > 0$ ($P = 100$ in ScienceWorld). The episode pair produces a tuple:
\begin{multline}
    \bigl(s^{\tt int}, s^{\tt det}, d^{\tt int}, d^{\tt det}\bigr) \triangleq \\
    \bigl(s(\tau_{\tt int}),\, s(\tau_{\tt det}),\, d(\tau_{\tt int}),\, d(\tau_{\tt det})\bigr)
\end{multline}

We use Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct~\citep{qwen25-2024} (vLLM) on ScienceWorld \cite{scienceworld-2022}, a text-based environment spanning 30 task types with a $-100$ death penalty. After prescreening, we collect 245 episode pairs across 11 task types (20--40 per type). The framework is applicable to any environment where paired episodes under two computation modes can be collected with binary success/failure indicators. We apply it unchanged to ALFWorld and MATH in \S\ref{sec:math-contrast}--\ref{sec:cross-env}.

\subsection{Episode-Level Labeling}
\label{sec:labeling}

We define a labeling function $L$ that maps a counterfactual pair to a routing preference:
\begin{multline}
    L\!\bigl(\tau_{\tt int}^{(t)}, \tau_{\tt det}^{(t)}\bigr) \;\rightarrow \\
    \ell \in \{{\tt det\text{-}pref},\; {\tt int\text{-}pref},\; {\tt no\text{-}pref}\}
\end{multline}

The labeling function is \emph{death-aware}: it prioritizes survival over score, then compares scores among same-status episodes (Algorithm~\ref{alg:labeling}; Appendix~\ref{app:labeling}). ScienceWorld scores are integer-valued, so we use exact (in)equality with no threshold. Figure~\ref{fig:pipeline} illustrates the overall pipeline; label distributions are reported in \S\ref{sec:death-decomp}.

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{figures/pipeline_overview.pdf}
\caption{Counterfactual episode collection and analysis pipeline. Each task instance is run under both pure strategies independently, producing episode-level labels via Algorithm~\ref{alg:labeling}.}
\label{fig:pipeline}
\end{figure}

\subsection{Death Avoidance Factor}
\label{sec:daf}

To quantify the contribution of death avoidance to routing signal, we decompose the mean score gap between strategies. Let $\delta = \mathbb{E}[d^{\tt int}] - \mathbb{E}[d^{\tt det}]$ denote the death-rate differential and $\Delta_{\text{nd}}$ the mean score gap restricted to episodes where neither strategy dies. Partitioning episodes by death status, death-asymmetric episodes (where only one strategy dies) contribute a per-episode gap of $P + \bar{s}_{\text{surv}}$, where $\bar{s}_{\text{surv}}$ is the surviving strategy's mean score; when $\bar{s}_{\text{surv}} \ll P$ (in our data, $\bar{s}_{\text{surv}} = 0.87$, or $0.9\%$ of $P$), these episodes contribute ${\approx}\,\delta P$ in aggregate. The full gap thus decomposes as:
\begin{equation}
    \Delta_{\text{full}} \approx \underbrace{\delta \cdot P}_{\text{death contribution}} + \Delta_{\text{nd}}
\end{equation}
The approximation residual is $29.60 - 29.37 = 0.23$ ($0.8\%$ of $\Delta_{\text{full}}$).

We define the Death Avoidance Factor (DAF) as the fraction of the full score gap attributable to death avoidance:
\begin{equation}
    \text{DAF} = 1 - \frac{\Delta_{\text{nd}}}{\Delta_{\text{full}}} \approx \frac{\delta P}{\delta P + \Delta_{\text{nd}}}
\end{equation}%
\footnote{When $\Delta_{\text{nd}} < 0$ (deterministic strategy scores lower than internal on non-death episodes), DAF exceeds 1; death avoidance then overcompensates for a non-death disadvantage.}

Since DAF $= \delta P / (\delta P + \Delta_{\text{nd}})$, it increases monotonically with $P$, a mathematical consequence rather than an empirical finding. We therefore report both DAF at the environment default ($P{=}100$) and the penalty-independent death-asymmetry label fraction as complementary metrics.

\paragraph{Statistical inference.}
We quantify uncertainty via episode-level bootstrap resampling ($B = 10{,}000$). Each resample draws $N = 245$ episodes with replacement, recomputes all metrics, and derives percentile-based 95\% CIs. We resample at the episode level (not the step level) because the episode is the unit of counterfactual comparison. Penalty sensitivity analysis across hypothetical $P$ values is in Appendix~\ref{app:penalty}.

We also measure whether death-avoidance signal is concentrated in a few task types via a Signal Concentration Index (SCI $\in [0, 1]$; Appendix~\ref{app:sci}), reported in \S\ref{sec:death-decomp}.

\paragraph{Scope of episode-level analysis.}
At step level, 97.4\% of oracle comparisons yield ties (\S\ref{sec:collection}) and only 0.3\% of training steps exhibit directional conflict with episode labels. This near-complete absence of step-level signal---rather than label noise---explains why episode-level analysis is the appropriate granularity and why step-level router training approaches chance performance (\S\ref{sec:router-diagnostic}).

